WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.855 --> 00:00:15.070
Now I'd like to introduce today's
featured presenter, Pamela Hinds.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:17.623
Pamela Hinds is a Professor
of Management Science and

00:00:17.623 --> 00:00:20.868
Engineering in the School of
Engineering at Stanford University.

00:00:20.868 --> 00:00:24.237
The Director of the Center for
Work Technology and Organization, and

00:00:24.237 --> 00:00:26.579
Faculty Director of
the School of Engineering,

00:00:26.579 --> 00:00:29.340
Global Engineering Program for students.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.460
Pamela studies the effect
of technology on groups.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:36.800
She's contributed extensive research on
the dynamics of geographically distributed

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.810
work teams, particularly those
spanning national boundaries.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:44.750
She explored issues of culture,
language, emotion and conflict, and

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:47.755
the role of site visits and
promoting trust and collaboration.

00:00:47.755 --> 00:00:52.050
Most recently, she has been exploring the
relationship between national culture and

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:55.615
work practices especially
innovation practices.

00:00:55.615 --> 00:00:59.325
Pamela has also been exploring the
relationship between national culture and

00:00:59.325 --> 00:01:00.635
technology use.

00:01:00.635 --> 00:01:04.015
And is interested in the design
of collaborative technologies for

00:01:04.015 --> 00:01:07.555
use across national boundaries.

00:01:07.555 --> 00:01:09.648
Now I'll turn it over to Pamela.

00:01:09.648 --> 00:01:13.688
&gt;&gt; Okay, as Sherry said,
I'm going to be talking a little bit today

00:01:13.688 --> 00:01:18.280
about Us vs Them, overcoming
differences to inspire collaboration.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:23.137
Many of you are probably working in
teams that are spending a variety of

00:01:23.137 --> 00:01:25.452
different kinds of boundaries.

00:01:25.452 --> 00:01:30.623
Working with people who are, maybe in
a different location, even in a different

00:01:30.623 --> 00:01:36.187
country, from a different discipline,
in a different organization, and so forth.

00:01:36.187 --> 00:01:40.890
And all of those differences can really
lead to positive outcomes, but can also be

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:45.206
challenging for people, and that's
what we're going to talk about today.

00:01:48.638 --> 00:01:55.653
So one premise, and this is something that
was mentioned by new article by Forbes.

00:01:55.653 --> 00:01:58.388
Diversity is a key driver for
innovation, and

00:01:58.388 --> 00:02:01.763
that is something that is
generally believed to be true.

00:02:01.763 --> 00:02:05.859
What we've seen over the last
couple of decades is that more and

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:10.655
more organizations are looking to
diversity as a way to bring in different

00:02:10.655 --> 00:02:14.392
perspectives, and
drive higher levels of innovation.

00:02:14.392 --> 00:02:21.230
There was a survey that was done by Forbes
of C-level executives around the globe.

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:26.575
And they asked them, does a diverse,
inclusive workforce, is it crucial to

00:02:26.575 --> 00:02:31.606
encouraging different perspectives and
ideas that drive innovation?

00:02:31.606 --> 00:02:39.950
48% of the people surveyed strongly agreed
with that statement, 37% somewhat agreed.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:45.345
So a very large percentage of the
respondents really do believe that having

00:02:45.345 --> 00:02:51.340
a diverse and inclusive workforce helps
bring in these different perspectives and

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:55.467
increase the innovation
potential of an organization.

00:02:57.745 --> 00:03:00.532
So, another quick poll.

00:03:00.532 --> 00:03:06.927
In your opinion, does working across
boundaries actually increase innovation?

00:03:06.927 --> 00:03:10.226
Well, I've got 10 seconds for
you to complete the poll.

00:03:10.226 --> 00:03:15.723
If you don't think it really helps at all,
you could answer one or A.

00:03:15.723 --> 00:03:19.842
If you think that it's really crucial for
innovation,

00:03:19.842 --> 00:03:22.567
you can answer E or five, very much.

00:03:25.437 --> 00:03:27.036
So the poll has ended.

00:03:28.363 --> 00:03:34.169
And, we're going to take just a few
seconds to pull up the results of that.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:36.853
Okay, here we go.

00:03:36.853 --> 00:03:41.716
And what we're seeing is
just like the executives

00:03:41.716 --> 00:03:45.200
that were polled around the globe.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:53.052
Many people seem to think that it probably
leads to higher levels of innovation.

00:03:53.052 --> 00:03:58.882
Or even you've got 127 respondents
that said that it's very important,

00:03:58.882 --> 00:04:01.489
it very much leads to innovation.

00:04:01.489 --> 00:04:04.379
So let's look at what the research shows.

00:04:05.658 --> 00:04:07.264
It actually depends.

00:04:07.264 --> 00:04:13.255
Does working across boundaries
increase innovation?

00:04:13.255 --> 00:04:17.890
Some of the research suggests
that there are real benefits.

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:20.160
And some of it suggests that, in fact,

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:23.700
we're not seeing higher
levels of innovation.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:27.450
There's work done by
Lee Fleming at Harvard.

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:32.830
And he looked in particular at diversity
in team members' disciplines or

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:34.928
backgrounds, their functional expertise.

00:04:34.928 --> 00:04:38.480
And then mapped that against

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:43.530
the value of innovation they
were producing in these teams.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:47.690
And there are two really important things
that you'll see if you look closely

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:48.690
at this graph.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:54.399
One is, that the average
value of innovation actually

00:04:54.399 --> 00:04:59.497
goes down as you get more
diversity on the teams.

00:04:59.497 --> 00:05:03.409
So that's a little bit surprising
given that we really think and

00:05:03.409 --> 00:05:09.080
hope that higher levels of diversity
should actually lead to more innovation.

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:12.340
But the other thing that's really
interesting about this slide

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:16.870
is that the only time you see
breakthrough innovations.

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:20.062
The only time that you
see really high level of

00:05:20.062 --> 00:05:24.099
innovation is when you have
this diversity in the teams.

00:05:25.263 --> 00:05:30.109
And what this tells us, and actually a lot
of research that's been done on a variety

00:05:30.109 --> 00:05:33.919
of different kinds of diversity,
including ethnic diversity,

00:05:33.919 --> 00:05:38.097
gender diversity, diversity in
national background, and so forth.

00:05:38.097 --> 00:05:42.804
All of that research shows that
oftentimes, you do see a decrease in

00:05:42.804 --> 00:05:48.761
innovation, and a decrease in performance
when you have a lot of diversity in teams.

00:05:48.761 --> 00:05:54.624
But, it's also an opportunity for
really high levels of innovation.

00:05:54.624 --> 00:05:57.470
But the challenge is managing those teams.

00:05:57.470 --> 00:06:00.124
It takes a little bit more effort and

00:06:00.124 --> 00:06:04.647
attention to manage teams with
higher levels of diversity.

00:06:14.442 --> 00:06:15.889
And a couple of elements.

00:06:15.889 --> 00:06:16.820
So first of all,

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:21.153
we've got a lot of different kinds of
diversity on the left side of the slide.

00:06:21.153 --> 00:06:25.759
And what really has to happen,
but what has to be managed in

00:06:25.759 --> 00:06:30.083
order to get the innovation
that a team is looking for,

00:06:30.083 --> 00:06:34.407
first is you have to actually
get the information out.

00:06:34.407 --> 00:06:39.010
The benefits of diversity is you
have these different perspectives.

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:43.063
You have different kinds of information
that people have access to.

00:06:43.063 --> 00:06:47.615
But sometimes it's a little bit
challenging to get all that information

00:06:47.615 --> 00:06:52.370
out in a way that it can be used
toward innovation and in the team.

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:57.190
And then the second element is once
you've got the information out,

00:06:57.190 --> 00:07:01.000
you see that people are seeing
these from different perspectives.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:03.250
How do you then integrate
those different perspectives?

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:05.653
How do you bring that together so

00:07:05.653 --> 00:07:09.931
that you're developing something
new as a result of it.

00:07:12.313 --> 00:07:18.051
So let's dive a little bit down into
the first one, getting information out.

00:07:18.051 --> 00:07:21.352
To achieve their innovation potential,

00:07:21.352 --> 00:07:26.360
teams need to first be able to
get the information on the table.

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:28.759
If you don't share these perspectives,

00:07:28.759 --> 00:07:32.265
then what's the point of working
across boundaries at all?

00:07:32.265 --> 00:07:35.360
Couple different things
that have to happen here.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:40.430
One is it really helps if the team
has a learning orientation.

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:42.660
And that's something that can
be influenced by the culture of

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:47.400
the organization, influenced by
the leader of the organization.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:51.690
And people really need to
want to learn from others.

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:56.630
They want to believe that learning these
different perspectives is actually going

00:07:56.630 --> 00:08:00.190
to help them to be more successful
in achieving their goals.

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:06.514
They also need to anticipate that there
are going to be alternative view points

00:08:06.514 --> 00:08:08.339
when they go into a meeting.

00:08:08.339 --> 00:08:12.789
It turns out that if people walk into
a meeting knowing that they're going

00:08:12.789 --> 00:08:17.264
to hear very different view points,
they come with a different mindset.

00:08:17.264 --> 00:08:21.002
And are better able to process
those different viewpoints.

00:08:21.002 --> 00:08:26.076
So letting people know that that's
valued and that they should expect that.

00:08:26.076 --> 00:08:29.270
And that's also really important
that people feel valued and safe.

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:33.140
People will not share their different
perspectives if they feel like their

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:38.300
perspective is not going to be
appreciated, heard, and so forth.

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:41.260
And then finally, it just takes more time.

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:45.173
When everybody shares the same
perspective, you've got a lot of common

00:08:45.173 --> 00:08:48.456
ground, it's really easy to
talk quickly through things.

00:08:48.456 --> 00:08:51.846
But when people have really
different perspectives,

00:08:51.846 --> 00:08:54.940
often much more explanation
is needed in order for

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:59.889
people to understand all those different
view points and that just takes time.

00:09:02.071 --> 00:09:04.845
And then once you got the information out,

00:09:04.845 --> 00:09:09.130
the next hurdle is integrating
this diverse information.

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:13.400
People need to understand the relevance
of what it is they've heard.

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:16.740
And if people are coming from
really different perspectives,

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:22.920
it can be challenging to see how
what somebody else is offering

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:27.660
fits the way that you're thinking about
the problem that you're working on.

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:31.710
And sometimes it can just
be hard to relate to and

00:09:31.710 --> 00:09:33.850
make sense of what
somebody else is saying.

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:37.230
A couple things that
are really helpful in that.

00:09:37.230 --> 00:09:39.916
One is, again, it takes time.

00:09:39.916 --> 00:09:44.568
There needs to be more conversation,
more discussion about these different

00:09:44.568 --> 00:09:47.294
perspectives in order
to see the relevance.

00:09:47.294 --> 00:09:51.562
In order to figure out where
there is common ground in

00:09:51.562 --> 00:09:56.524
the different viewpoints that
are being brought together.

00:09:56.524 --> 00:10:00.495
And that helps to overcome what's
called a representational gap.

00:10:00.495 --> 00:10:04.819
Which is that when people particularly
are coming from different

00:10:04.819 --> 00:10:07.728
disciplinary perspectives they often have

00:10:07.728 --> 00:10:12.220
different ways that they have been
trained to represent a problem.

00:10:13.510 --> 00:10:18.586
And again, if you can take the time for
people to really articulate how they're

00:10:18.586 --> 00:10:23.894
representing the problem, how they're
prioritizing different elements of it,

00:10:23.894 --> 00:10:28.251
then that can help people to find
opportunities for common ground.

00:10:30.462 --> 00:10:35.113
So what we're going to focus on
today is the us versus them dynamic

00:10:35.113 --> 00:10:38.547
that interferes with
all of these processes.

00:10:38.547 --> 00:10:42.501
So that's one of the things
that as leaders,

00:10:42.501 --> 00:10:46.150
you can work on addressing in your teams.

00:10:46.150 --> 00:10:50.192
And that's effective whether
you're cross organizational,

00:10:50.192 --> 00:10:54.695
cross functional, cross geography,
cross cultural, and so forth.

00:10:58.386 --> 00:11:03.614
It turns out, that the us vs them dynamic
that you see in these teams is one

00:11:03.614 --> 00:11:08.710
of the most pervasive problems that
we see in cross-boundary teams.

00:11:08.710 --> 00:11:12.560
It's one of these things that is just so
human.

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:14.340
We have this natural tendency

00:11:15.350 --> 00:11:19.910
to subgroup into different groups
that are similar to ourselves.

00:11:19.910 --> 00:11:23.665
And this is something that just
naturally happens in these teams.

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:30.390
And I'll talk a little bit today about why
that happens and what we can do about it.

00:11:30.390 --> 00:11:33.390
But first, I'm going to ask
you a couple of questions and

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:35.290
then I'll just wait a few seconds.

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:39.100
You might want to just write
down a couple of your answers.

00:11:39.100 --> 00:11:43.990
So each of these, I'd like you to answer
why you think this might have happened.

00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:49.792
So the first one is a colleague
that you're working with in

00:11:49.792 --> 00:11:54.776
Amsterdam regularly gets up and
leaves your teleconferences

00:11:54.776 --> 00:11:59.683
early at 4:50 PM their time,
instead of 5 o'clock PM.

00:11:59.683 --> 00:12:03.903
So just make a couple of notes
about why they might be doing this.

00:12:06.999 --> 00:12:10.867
All right, and
then the next one a coworker from

00:12:10.867 --> 00:12:14.746
marketing gives you
misleading information.

00:12:14.746 --> 00:12:16.758
Why might that have happened?

00:12:18.265 --> 00:12:23.236
Again, just take a few seconds
to jot down a couple notes.

00:12:25.591 --> 00:12:30.351
All right, the third one is a colleague
from another company that you're

00:12:30.351 --> 00:12:34.130
working with sends you
an analysis that's badly flawed.

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:36.697
Why might that have happened?

00:12:41.261 --> 00:12:45.390
Okay, so for each of these,
there are many possible responses.

00:12:45.390 --> 00:12:50.440
For the colleague in Amsterdam,
you might've said that they had a train

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:55.730
to catch and it only leaves hourly and
they need to get home to their family.

00:12:55.730 --> 00:12:59.440
You might have said that they're lazy,
that they're uncommitted.

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:04.050
These are all answer that I've
gotten before to this question.

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:09.330
For the second one, you might say
that the person is incompetent.

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:11.800
So they gave you misleading information.

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:14.970
Or they've got another
agenda that they're working.

00:13:14.970 --> 00:13:19.780
You might have also said that they just
have a different way of explaining things

00:13:19.780 --> 00:13:21.440
because they're from a different field.

00:13:22.990 --> 00:13:28.740
For the third one,
you might have said that the person

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:33.860
misunderstood the request, or they didn't
have the information that they needed.

00:13:33.860 --> 00:13:36.240
So these are all what
we call attributions,

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:41.540
they're ways of explaining
things when things go wrong.

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:46.015
And we are making attributions all day,
every day.

00:13:46.015 --> 00:13:49.980
We're making judgements about why it
is that something happened the way

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:50.700
that it did.

00:13:57.027 --> 00:14:02.182
So there's a whole theory in
psychology around attribution theory.

00:14:02.182 --> 00:14:07.101
And what attribution theory suggests is
that there are two different types of

00:14:07.101 --> 00:14:09.152
attributions that we can make.

00:14:09.152 --> 00:14:12.518
We can make what are called
dispositional attributions and

00:14:12.518 --> 00:14:16.190
those are attributions
that are about the person.

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:20.610
So if you found yourself writing
things like they're lazy,

00:14:20.610 --> 00:14:23.720
uncommitted, incompetent,
anything of that sort.

00:14:23.720 --> 00:14:25.929
Those would be dispositional or

00:14:25.929 --> 00:14:29.867
personal attributions about
why something went wrong.

00:14:29.867 --> 00:14:32.336
But then there's also
situational attributions.

00:14:32.336 --> 00:14:36.656
And situational attributions
are attributing what it is

00:14:36.656 --> 00:14:40.803
that went wrong to
characteristics of the situation.

00:14:40.803 --> 00:14:44.366
So if you wrote things like they
need to catch the train, or

00:14:44.366 --> 00:14:49.314
they didn't have the information that
they should have been given and so forth,

00:14:49.314 --> 00:14:52.980
those are more situational attributions.

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:54.520
And as you can imagine,

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:58.240
the situational attributions tend
to be a little bit more generous.

00:14:59.550 --> 00:15:04.252
The thing is that we tend
to lean toward making

00:15:04.252 --> 00:15:09.219
more dispositional
attributions in general.

00:15:09.219 --> 00:15:13.372
And the reason that we make more
dispositional attributions, or

00:15:13.372 --> 00:15:18.356
personal attributions is because we
don't have as much information about why

00:15:18.356 --> 00:15:20.860
people are doing what they're doing.

00:15:20.860 --> 00:15:23.610
We have a lot of information about
why we're doing what we're doing.

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:25.970
But not so much with other people.

00:15:25.970 --> 00:15:29.784
And then if they are in a different
location or in a different country,

00:15:29.784 --> 00:15:33.857
if they're in a different culture,
in a different firm, we have even less

00:15:33.857 --> 00:15:38.083
information to help us explain why it
is they're doing what they're doing.

00:15:38.083 --> 00:15:42.923
So there's a natural tendency
in these cross-boundary

00:15:42.923 --> 00:15:47.255
teams to actually make more
of these kind of harsh,

00:15:47.255 --> 00:15:54.334
personal kinds of attributions and that
really fuels this us versus them dynamic.

00:15:54.334 --> 00:15:59.630
There's an ingroup bias as well which
I'll talk a little bit about in a minute.

00:16:01.710 --> 00:16:03.652
So what can you do as leaders?

00:16:03.652 --> 00:16:08.464
When you see this us versus
them dynamic and I have to say,

00:16:08.464 --> 00:16:10.678
it is almost inevitable.

00:16:10.678 --> 00:16:13.204
You probably will see it, but

00:16:13.204 --> 00:16:18.670
you can also do things to reduce
the likelihood of it showing up.

00:16:18.670 --> 00:16:20.643
What are those?

00:16:20.643 --> 00:16:22.645
There are three things
I'm going to talk about.

00:16:22.645 --> 00:16:25.110
One is shared identity.

00:16:25.110 --> 00:16:29.400
One is trust and then the third
is psychological safety, and

00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:31.780
these are all really intertwined.

00:16:31.780 --> 00:16:34.730
They're all mutually
supportive of one another.

00:16:34.730 --> 00:16:37.130
So there's not one that's more
important than the other.

00:16:37.130 --> 00:16:41.794
You could actually start at
any one of these three and

00:16:41.794 --> 00:16:46.988
that would help to erode some
of the us versus them dynamics

00:16:46.988 --> 00:16:51.763
that you are seeing or
that could happen in your team.

00:16:51.763 --> 00:16:53.810
So we'll start with shared identity.

00:16:55.160 --> 00:16:59.908
This is just some research that I
did a while back with Mark Mortensen

00:16:59.908 --> 00:17:01.922
who's at INSEAD in France.

00:17:01.922 --> 00:17:05.841
We were looking at globally
distributed teams and

00:17:05.841 --> 00:17:10.972
one of the things that we found
was that having a shared identity,

00:17:10.972 --> 00:17:17.054
significantly reduced the amount of
conflict that occurred on these teams.

00:17:17.054 --> 00:17:20.625
And in particular,
something called affective conflict and

00:17:20.625 --> 00:17:22.968
that's where it really gets personal.

00:17:22.968 --> 00:17:27.568
So having a shared identity
really help to reduce those

00:17:27.568 --> 00:17:32.468
negative attributions about
the person in particular and

00:17:32.468 --> 00:17:37.568
there's a ton of research in
social psychology that looks at

00:17:37.568 --> 00:17:42.470
the importance of shared identity,
and having a vision.

00:17:42.470 --> 00:17:46.250
This is something that you can
do in your teams as leaders.

00:17:46.250 --> 00:17:48.440
If people have a shared vision,

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:52.340
they believe that they
are working toward a common goal.

00:17:52.340 --> 00:17:53.936
That they are all in it together.

00:17:53.936 --> 00:17:56.293
That they are interdependent and

00:17:56.293 --> 00:18:01.847
have to rely on one another in order to
be successful at achieving their goals,

00:18:01.847 --> 00:18:06.912
then you see a shared identity
develop very rapidly, quite strongly.

00:18:06.912 --> 00:18:12.093
And the us versus them dynamics
can virtually disappear,

00:18:12.093 --> 00:18:17.074
if people are really focused
on a strong shared vision.

00:18:17.074 --> 00:18:21.906
Now, the trick is that you can start
this at the beginning of a project.

00:18:21.906 --> 00:18:24.030
People completely bought in.

00:18:24.030 --> 00:18:27.910
Everybody's really aligned with the vision
and see how other's fit in, and

00:18:27.910 --> 00:18:30.080
why they need to work together.

00:18:30.080 --> 00:18:34.721
But when you're working across boundaries,
it erodes more rapidly.

00:18:34.721 --> 00:18:36.579
If you are not co-located,

00:18:36.579 --> 00:18:40.457
people begin to make sense of
the vision in different ways.

00:18:40.457 --> 00:18:43.203
If you are from different disciplines,

00:18:43.203 --> 00:18:47.009
you may start to prioritize
one element over the other.

00:18:47.009 --> 00:18:52.406
So, it's something that has to be
completely or continuously revisited and

00:18:52.406 --> 00:18:57.813
reinforced in order to ensure that that
is still in place on an ongoing basis.

00:19:00.050 --> 00:19:02.650
So now, we're going to talk
a little bit about trust.

00:19:02.650 --> 00:19:04.767
And again, these are intertwined.

00:19:04.767 --> 00:19:09.262
So it turns out that if you got
a stronger shared identity,

00:19:09.262 --> 00:19:12.034
what comes with that is more trust.

00:19:12.034 --> 00:19:16.862
And if you got more trust,
it's easier to build a shared identity.

00:19:16.862 --> 00:19:24.290
So with trust, I'd like you to bring
to mind somebody that you really trust.

00:19:24.290 --> 00:19:27.630
So just for a second, think of somebody.

00:19:29.610 --> 00:19:33.593
And then again, just jot down a couple of

00:19:33.593 --> 00:19:38.877
characteristics that lead
you to trust this person.

00:19:38.877 --> 00:19:40.992
I'll give you a few seconds
to write those down.

00:19:46.324 --> 00:19:51.921
So you might have written a whole host
of things like they care about you.

00:19:51.921 --> 00:19:54.774
They always do what they say.

00:19:54.774 --> 00:19:56.921
They know what they're doing.

00:19:56.921 --> 00:20:00.103
I'm sure there's a whole variety
of things that you wrote down.

00:20:00.103 --> 00:20:03.504
What I'm going to show you now
is definition of trust and

00:20:03.504 --> 00:20:08.794
then what some of the different elements
are that have been found to compose trust,

00:20:08.794 --> 00:20:10.320
and trustworthiness.

00:20:11.850 --> 00:20:16.240
So trust is assured reliance
on the character, ability or

00:20:16.240 --> 00:20:18.730
truth of someone or something.

00:20:18.730 --> 00:20:23.825
Somebody in which you put confidence and
a more research-oriented definition.

00:20:23.825 --> 00:20:28.599
The willingness to be vulnerable to
the actions of another person based on

00:20:28.599 --> 00:20:30.139
the expectation that,

00:20:30.139 --> 00:20:35.221
that other person will perform a
particular action that's important to you,

00:20:35.221 --> 00:20:40.398
the trustor, irrespective of your ability
to monitor or control that person.

00:20:40.398 --> 00:20:42.437
So there's a couple of things I
want to pull out of that definition.

00:20:42.437 --> 00:20:47.000
One is trust is really
about being vulnerable.

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:50.830
So it's only really trust if you've

00:20:50.830 --> 00:20:56.150
ended up in a situation where you
actually depend upon that person and

00:20:56.150 --> 00:21:00.880
you trust them to actually do it is you
need done, and they've said they would do.

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:06.646
And importantly, it's irrespective of the
ability to monitor or control the person.

00:21:06.646 --> 00:21:11.088
Some people say that you trust,
but control.

00:21:11.088 --> 00:21:13.695
The truth is they're different things.

00:21:13.695 --> 00:21:18.696
And oftentimes, when you are especially
geographically distributed,

00:21:18.696 --> 00:21:21.767
it's really difficult to monitor control.

00:21:21.767 --> 00:21:26.330
So trust becomes even more important in
these situations and that's also true when

00:21:26.330 --> 00:21:30.837
you're crossing other kinds of boundaries,
when you're working across firm.

00:21:30.837 --> 00:21:36.129
It's true when you're working across
disciplines where you actually

00:21:36.129 --> 00:21:41.420
don't have enough knowledge of that
discipline to evaluate whether or

00:21:41.420 --> 00:21:45.300
not what they're doing is appropriate or
correct,

00:21:45.300 --> 00:21:50.436
then you really are vulnerable their
actions and trust is required.

00:21:50.436 --> 00:21:56.566
So there are three elements of trust
that have been identified in research.

00:21:56.566 --> 00:21:58.491
One is benevolence.

00:21:58.491 --> 00:22:02.031
So benevolence is really about whether or

00:22:02.031 --> 00:22:06.284
not the person is caring and
concerned about you.

00:22:06.284 --> 00:22:11.368
Benevolence at the extreme is
they will do something for

00:22:11.368 --> 00:22:16.570
you even if they have to make
a sacrifice for themselves.

00:22:16.570 --> 00:22:20.982
So if you said that I trust this person,
because they care about me.

00:22:20.982 --> 00:22:24.159
I know they'll do the right thing,
then that's really benevolence.

00:22:25.700 --> 00:22:27.306
The next one is ability and

00:22:27.306 --> 00:22:30.817
this is something that people
often don't think about.

00:22:30.817 --> 00:22:35.703
But particularly in a work context,
we have to believe that people

00:22:35.703 --> 00:22:40.345
can actually do what it is they
need to do in order to trust them.

00:22:40.345 --> 00:22:42.392
They have to have the technical skills.

00:22:42.392 --> 00:22:47.907
They have to have the marketing savvy in
order for us to trust that they're going

00:22:47.907 --> 00:22:53.112
to be able to fulfill their obligations
to us and then there's integrity.

00:22:53.112 --> 00:22:59.895
An integrity is really about reliability,
predictability, honor.

00:22:59.895 --> 00:23:05.880
Do they have a code by which they
are operating that you can count on.

00:23:05.880 --> 00:23:09.741
It doesn't necessarily need to be the same
code as yours, but you can count on it.

00:23:09.741 --> 00:23:13.535
You know what it is to expect of them and

00:23:13.535 --> 00:23:19.117
the three of these compose trust and
trustworthiness.

00:23:19.117 --> 00:23:23.131
A couple of things that are sort of
interesting to know is that different

00:23:23.131 --> 00:23:26.136
people value the different
elements differently.

00:23:26.136 --> 00:23:29.937
So you may be somebody where your whole
list was all about benevolence, but

00:23:29.937 --> 00:23:32.971
somebody else might really
think it's all about ability.

00:23:32.971 --> 00:23:36.341
And they really want to work with
people that are the most capable and

00:23:36.341 --> 00:23:38.642
they don't worry too
much about the others.

00:23:38.642 --> 00:23:43.364
So it's not a bad conversation
to have to understand and

00:23:43.364 --> 00:23:48.287
what people are really valuing,
and to use that as a lens to

00:23:48.287 --> 00:23:53.430
evaluate whether or
not you can assess them as trustworthy.

00:23:55.931 --> 00:24:00.450
So if we're talking about cross-boundary
teams, this is our next poll.

00:24:00.450 --> 00:24:04.450
Is there generally less trust
on cross-boundary teams?

00:24:04.450 --> 00:24:06.200
Or more trust?

00:24:06.200 --> 00:24:11.111
So we'll take about ten seconds for
you to answer this poll.

00:24:11.111 --> 00:24:15.510
And this can be any kind
of cross-boundary team.

00:24:15.510 --> 00:24:19.953
The poll has ended, and we're going to
wait just a couple of seconds while

00:24:19.953 --> 00:24:22.767
we get the results of
the poll to see whether or

00:24:22.767 --> 00:24:27.900
not people think there's going to be more
or less, or no difference on these teams.

00:24:29.740 --> 00:24:31.613
All right, well, this is interesting.

00:24:31.613 --> 00:24:36.506
So most people,
231 of the respondents thought that

00:24:36.506 --> 00:24:41.730
there would be less trust on
these cross-boundary teams.

00:24:41.730 --> 00:24:43.840
A handful thought it would
be about the same, and

00:24:43.840 --> 00:24:48.380
a few people thought you actually could
end up with more trust on these teams.

00:24:50.080 --> 00:24:55.820
So we thought that there would
be less trust on these teams and

00:24:55.820 --> 00:25:00.650
set out to do a research study to see
the extent to which that was true.

00:25:00.650 --> 00:25:04.335
And what we found was, in fact,
it was about the same.

00:25:04.335 --> 00:25:09.644
So these, in particular,
were geographically co-located versus

00:25:09.644 --> 00:25:14.305
distributed teams and
they were construction engineering teams.

00:25:14.305 --> 00:25:17.470
So we had some architects,
some people in construction,

00:25:17.470 --> 00:25:22.490
and variety of other disciplines in there.

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:29.830
So they were geographically distributed
and also from different disciplines.

00:25:29.830 --> 00:25:34.919
But as you can see, we looked at month
one when they formed these teams and

00:25:34.919 --> 00:25:39.109
month three and
it didn't really seem to matter very much.

00:25:39.109 --> 00:25:40.970
So that kind of surprised us and

00:25:40.970 --> 00:25:44.699
we dug a little deeper into
the data to see what was going on.

00:25:44.699 --> 00:25:51.830
And what we saw was that on the collocated
teams, it was much more volatile.

00:25:51.830 --> 00:25:55.960
So we ended up with dramatic
increases in trust, but

00:25:55.960 --> 00:25:58.510
also dramatic decreases in trust.

00:25:58.510 --> 00:26:02.369
And we think that had a lot to do with
the fact that when you're colocated,

00:26:02.369 --> 00:26:05.150
you can actually see
people's' behaviors more.

00:26:05.150 --> 00:26:10.740
You've got more information about whether
or not they are caring toward other

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:15.050
people, whether or not they are capable,
whether or not they do have integrity.

00:26:16.950 --> 00:26:23.090
So we got increases and decreases as
people adjusted their level of trust.

00:26:23.090 --> 00:26:26.680
In the distributive teams,
there was a lot less adjustment.

00:26:26.680 --> 00:26:32.248
People started with a certain level
of trust at the beginning and

00:26:32.248 --> 00:26:35.247
that was maintained throughout.

00:26:35.247 --> 00:26:38.911
And looking at this a little bit more
closely what we saw was in collocated

00:26:38.911 --> 00:26:41.915
teams, people paid attention
to the behavior, whether or

00:26:41.915 --> 00:26:46.880
not people were following through,
their team members were following through.

00:26:46.880 --> 00:26:49.900
And that affected whether or
not they were trustworthy.

00:26:49.900 --> 00:26:53.340
And then that affected their
trust later in the process.

00:26:54.660 --> 00:26:59.020
But in the distributed teams,
it started with perceived trustworthiness.

00:26:59.020 --> 00:27:04.120
So very quickly people,
team members evaluated whether or

00:27:04.120 --> 00:27:06.200
not somebody was trustworthy.

00:27:06.200 --> 00:27:08.760
Then when they saw their behaviors,

00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:13.730
they looked at those behaviors through
that lens of being trustworthy or not.

00:27:13.730 --> 00:27:16.610
And then the attributions
were aligned with whether or

00:27:16.610 --> 00:27:18.760
not they thought they were trustworthy.

00:27:18.760 --> 00:27:22.340
And that affected trust
later on in the game.

00:27:22.340 --> 00:27:27.830
So basically what this shows
is that establishing trust

00:27:27.830 --> 00:27:31.810
really early in these
cross-boundary teams is crucial.

00:27:31.810 --> 00:27:37.326
Because once it gets established,
the updating doesn't happen as dynamically

00:27:37.326 --> 00:27:42.598
as it does on teams where there's a lot
more similarity and in particular when

00:27:42.598 --> 00:27:47.969
people are collocated and have more
information about what people are doing.

00:27:47.969 --> 00:27:52.871
So now we'll talk a little bit
about psychological safety, and

00:27:52.871 --> 00:27:57.430
psychological safety is pretty
closely related to trust.

00:27:57.430 --> 00:28:02.380
When you have more trust people
feel more safe psychologically,

00:28:02.380 --> 00:28:06.870
and if you've got more psychological
safety, people are more trusting and

00:28:06.870 --> 00:28:08.990
then the same thing with shared identity.

00:28:10.730 --> 00:28:15.595
So psychological safety is
defined as a shared belief.

00:28:15.595 --> 00:28:21.230
That's held by a team that the team is
safe for interpersonal risk taking.

00:28:21.230 --> 00:28:25.280
This was an idea that was
developed by Amy Edmondson

00:28:25.280 --> 00:28:28.090
at Harvard Business School
about twenty years ago.

00:28:28.090 --> 00:28:32.150
There's been a ton of research on
psychological safety now establishing it.

00:28:33.220 --> 00:28:36.100
As an important predictor
of team success and

00:28:36.100 --> 00:28:40.210
particularly team innovation in
many places around the globe.

00:28:41.400 --> 00:28:46.830
The basic idea here is
that people need to feel

00:28:46.830 --> 00:28:52.250
safe sharing unpopular opinions.

00:28:52.250 --> 00:28:58.680
Sharing knowledge, pointing out errors,
talking about mistakes that they've made.

00:28:58.680 --> 00:29:03.300
It's basically an environment where
people feel like they are supported

00:29:03.300 --> 00:29:07.030
by one another, and
there's not competition and

00:29:07.030 --> 00:29:10.760
undermining going on by
members of the team.

00:29:10.760 --> 00:29:14.260
So this is really crucial for
innovation in general and

00:29:14.260 --> 00:29:16.770
particularly when you have
cross-boundary teams.

00:29:18.730 --> 00:29:23.598
Psychological safety has been
shown to have significant impacts

00:29:23.598 --> 00:29:27.316
on team performance,
on team problem solving and

00:29:27.316 --> 00:29:31.220
particularly on learning and
innovation in teams.

00:29:33.674 --> 00:29:37.997
And the interesting thing about
psychological safety is that it is

00:29:37.997 --> 00:29:41.020
significantly affected by leader behavior.

00:29:41.020 --> 00:29:43.610
That is one of the number
one predictors of whether or

00:29:43.610 --> 00:29:46.650
not you'll have psychological safety,
is whether or

00:29:46.650 --> 00:29:52.930
not the leader sort of sets the tone for
people to feel comfortable.

00:29:52.930 --> 00:29:55.140
And again,
this has been done around the globe.

00:29:57.080 --> 00:30:02.240
For example,
when leaders are open, accessible,

00:30:02.240 --> 00:30:09.290
available to the people that they're
working with when they model

00:30:09.290 --> 00:30:15.020
psychological safe behaviors, they
talk about mistakes that they've made.

00:30:15.020 --> 00:30:19.762
They don't punish or
degrade people who have failed, but

00:30:19.762 --> 00:30:24.988
really use that as a learning opportunity,
all of that behavior

00:30:24.988 --> 00:30:29.951
by leaders leads to higher
levels of psychological safety.

00:30:29.951 --> 00:30:34.950
And then, one additional practice I wanted
to share with you is team reflection.

00:30:34.950 --> 00:30:40.100
So this can really help increase
psychological safety, as well as trust.

00:30:40.100 --> 00:30:45.290
Where teams actually take 15 minutes

00:30:45.290 --> 00:30:50.600
on occasion, maybe once a month or so,
to just reflect on how things are going.

00:30:51.720 --> 00:30:53.280
Are they happy with their performance?

00:30:53.280 --> 00:30:54.410
Are they happy with the process?

00:30:54.410 --> 00:30:57.330
Are they happy with the way
that they're working together?

00:30:57.330 --> 00:31:00.837
How might they improve their
ability to work together?

00:31:00.837 --> 00:31:06.232
What are the strategies that they might
use in order to get better performance and

00:31:06.232 --> 00:31:08.386
feel better about the process.

00:31:08.386 --> 00:31:11.450
The interesting thing is it's sort
of like exercising regularly.

00:31:11.450 --> 00:31:15.830
Even though you know it's good for you,
it doesn't always happen spontaneously.

00:31:15.830 --> 00:31:17.640
People are really busy.

00:31:17.640 --> 00:31:20.600
People are focused on the work
that they need to get done.

00:31:20.600 --> 00:31:25.820
So it is really important that
a context be set for this to happen.

00:31:25.820 --> 00:31:31.340
Where the leader, for example, says,
we are going to do this every month or

00:31:31.340 --> 00:31:34.260
at some best by interval.

00:31:34.260 --> 00:31:36.520
And time is actually made for it.

00:31:36.520 --> 00:31:41.350
Reflective teams are more effective, but
you gotta make the commitment to that.

00:31:43.945 --> 00:31:46.635
So just to sort of summarize
what we've talked about here.

00:31:46.635 --> 00:31:53.235
Shared identity is a really
powerful condition for teens.

00:31:53.235 --> 00:31:57.065
And that can be established
With a common vision,

00:31:57.065 --> 00:32:01.185
making sure that people understand
their interdependences,

00:32:01.185 --> 00:32:06.717
what it is they're working on together,
and continue to reinforce that over time.

00:32:06.717 --> 00:32:10.875
With trust,
be vigilant against harsh attributions.

00:32:10.875 --> 00:32:15.520
One of the ways to do this is to
always seek clarifying information.

00:32:15.520 --> 00:32:19.950
If you find yourself making
harsh attributions about

00:32:19.950 --> 00:32:24.130
particularly people in these
cross boundary situations.

00:32:25.200 --> 00:32:28.205
Or you see team members
making harsh attributions,

00:32:28.205 --> 00:32:30.194
personal kinds of attributions.

00:32:30.194 --> 00:32:33.855
It's always good to ask the question,
well what else might be going on here.

00:32:33.855 --> 00:32:37.169
What other information
could I get to help me

00:32:37.169 --> 00:32:41.930
to understand what really is
the reason behind this behavior.

00:32:41.930 --> 00:32:47.355
And that can really defuse the harsh
attributions and increase trust.

00:32:47.355 --> 00:32:52.217
And then, we forget psychological
safety a leader can be a role

00:32:52.217 --> 00:32:56.989
model by discussion mistakes,
being respectful of others,

00:32:56.989 --> 00:33:03.870
not punishing failure, expression kind
concern about employees and so forth.

00:33:03.870 --> 00:33:06.673
And then team reflection
can help with all of these.

00:33:10.036 --> 00:33:13.726
All right, so
to wrap up on cross-boundary teaming,

00:33:13.726 --> 00:33:18.944
we've talked about some of the core
issues that occur when people are working

00:33:18.944 --> 00:33:23.538
across these boundaries and
these us versus them dynamics emerge.

00:33:23.538 --> 00:33:28.802
And hopefully I've convinced you
that you can sustain higher levels

00:33:28.802 --> 00:33:33.793
of performance and
innovation by fostering, shared identity,

00:33:33.793 --> 00:33:37.708
psychological safety and
higher levels of trust.

00:33:37.708 --> 00:33:43.170
So thank you very much,
we've got some time for questions.

00:33:43.170 --> 00:33:48.290
I'm going to turn it over to Sherry,
who is going to facilitate that process.

00:33:50.310 --> 00:33:54.142
&gt;&gt; So let's see, so
from our participants, here's one.

00:33:54.142 --> 00:33:58.459
What's your advice for teams Pamela that
have very different business styles, and

00:33:58.459 --> 00:34:00.050
some of which may be cultural?

00:34:00.050 --> 00:34:03.780
Here's an example, specifically
we work with a team in Frankfort

00:34:03.780 --> 00:34:07.100
that's very cautious, methodical,
and adverse to failure.

00:34:07.100 --> 00:34:10.939
Our scientists and engineers in San Jose
are more aggressive with innovation and

00:34:10.939 --> 00:34:14.100
willing to accept small failures
to advance more quickly to a final

00:34:14.100 --> 00:34:15.124
successful design.

00:34:15.124 --> 00:34:16.903
How do they compromise?

00:34:16.903 --> 00:34:18.803
&gt;&gt; Right,
yeah that's a really good question.

00:34:18.803 --> 00:34:23.296
Is something that I talk about much
more extensively in the course?

00:34:23.296 --> 00:34:29.110
The cultural differences and
in particular, differences in practices.

00:34:29.110 --> 00:34:33.516
One of the things that I found in
my research where we've looked at

00:34:33.516 --> 00:34:38.326
innovation and innovation practices
in different parts of the world,

00:34:38.326 --> 00:34:42.180
is that there are different
approaches to innovation.

00:34:42.180 --> 00:34:47.680
And in fact what we've found is that some
of the things like rapid iteration that

00:34:47.680 --> 00:34:53.180
is so touted in the Silicon Valley
in the US culture

00:34:53.180 --> 00:34:57.700
actually can have negative effect
in some other cultural context.

00:34:57.700 --> 00:35:02.980
So I think it's really important
to know that what works for

00:35:02.980 --> 00:35:06.720
innovation in one culture may
not work in another culture.

00:35:06.720 --> 00:35:13.488
And I think the first thing to do is to
figure out what the best practices are for

00:35:13.488 --> 00:35:20.907
innovation in the culture in which you're
working, because it may not be the same.

00:35:20.907 --> 00:35:26.881
And then figure out how to,
I don't know if you want to compromise or

00:35:26.881 --> 00:35:31.577
if you want to allow multiple
practices to co-exist

00:35:31.577 --> 00:35:35.970
in order to get the most
out of different people.

00:35:35.970 --> 00:35:40.733
Or maybe try out different practices and
see what that generates.

00:35:40.733 --> 00:35:46.344
But I do think that it's really
important to start with the practice and

00:35:46.344 --> 00:35:50.927
understand how those are differ
in different cultures and

00:35:50.927 --> 00:35:55.980
not only national cultures but
also in functional cultures.

00:35:55.980 --> 00:35:59.838
Different professions have very,
very different practices.

00:35:59.838 --> 00:36:06.056
And really understanding those can help
to figure out how to work together,

00:36:06.056 --> 00:36:11.417
and achieve high level of innovation
in a united way, thank you.

00:36:11.417 --> 00:36:13.570
&gt;&gt; We have another participant asking,

00:36:13.570 --> 00:36:16.204
do you think trust needs
evidence to be built?

00:36:18.350 --> 00:36:20.740
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
that's really an interesting question.

00:36:20.740 --> 00:36:26.515
There is some research that talks
about something called swift trust.

00:36:26.515 --> 00:36:30.120
So swift trust does not
need evidence to be built.

00:36:30.120 --> 00:36:34.900
Swift trust happens because you
got information that gives you

00:36:34.900 --> 00:36:38.460
confidence that this
person should be trusted.

00:36:38.460 --> 00:36:41.280
So, for example,

00:36:41.280 --> 00:36:46.350
it's somebody that's been recommended to
you by somebody else that you trust a lot.

00:36:46.350 --> 00:36:51.850
Or they've gone to a top tier
university and gotten good grades.

00:36:51.850 --> 00:36:55.930
Well, then you might automatically
think that they're going

00:36:55.930 --> 00:36:58.800
to be a really competent, capable person.

00:36:58.800 --> 00:37:01.200
So there's a lot of information that we

00:37:01.200 --> 00:37:06.020
look to in our environment to figure out
whether or not somebody should be trusted.

00:37:06.020 --> 00:37:11.170
And we can look at that evidence
to build this swift trust.

00:37:11.170 --> 00:37:14.930
Or maybe somebody's licensed
in a particular field,

00:37:17.420 --> 00:37:20.580
so there is this with trust.

00:37:20.580 --> 00:37:22.470
It doesn't last forever, though.

00:37:22.470 --> 00:37:24.260
As you are working with somebody,

00:37:24.260 --> 00:37:29.420
you are always getting evidence
about trust and trustworthiness.

00:37:29.420 --> 00:37:34.590
So yes, certainly over time,
you do need evidence

00:37:34.590 --> 00:37:39.410
in order to I wouldn't say
determine your level of trust but

00:37:39.410 --> 00:37:43.670
adjust your level of initial
trust that was formed.

00:37:43.670 --> 00:37:45.550
The challenge is that we,

00:37:47.690 --> 00:37:52.470
the way that we process that
information can sometimes be biased

00:37:52.470 --> 00:37:57.220
because we don't have full information and
these attributions are at play.

00:37:57.220 --> 00:37:59.740
So we may see evidence of something.

00:37:59.740 --> 00:38:05.340
For example,
somebody not delivering a report on time.

00:38:05.340 --> 00:38:10.236
And we may then have our trust
lowered as a result of that,

00:38:10.236 --> 00:38:15.345
when in fact there might have
been something else going on.

00:38:15.345 --> 00:38:17.280
So really important that
you know the trust and

00:38:17.280 --> 00:38:19.610
the attributions are really intertwined.

00:38:19.610 --> 00:38:24.370
And one of the things that I really
encourage on these cross-boundary

00:38:24.370 --> 00:38:28.270
teams is to give people
the benefit of the doubt.

00:38:28.270 --> 00:38:33.870
To trust them first, and then figure out
what it is that might have been going on.

00:38:35.350 --> 00:38:36.626
&gt;&gt; Great, here's one,

00:38:36.626 --> 00:38:41.004
building trust with an overseas team
in Europe has become very challenging.

00:38:41.004 --> 00:38:43.070
The flow of information is one way.

00:38:43.070 --> 00:38:45.093
I don't get any information
back from them.

00:38:45.093 --> 00:38:47.136
What are the options this
participant might have?

00:38:51.594 --> 00:38:55.300
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's another really
good question, tough situation.

00:38:56.480 --> 00:39:01.053
And it's not uncommon to
have these situations where

00:39:01.053 --> 00:39:05.526
there's not enough
communication between teams or

00:39:05.526 --> 00:39:10.826
sub-teams that are located in
different parts of the globe.

00:39:10.826 --> 00:39:12.869
I guess a couple of things I'd try.

00:39:12.869 --> 00:39:17.210
One is that's more likely to happen.

00:39:17.210 --> 00:39:20.200
If there isn't a strong
sense of interdependence,

00:39:22.540 --> 00:39:26.200
if they really rely on you for
their success

00:39:28.400 --> 00:39:32.100
then they should be in contact
with you more frequently.

00:39:32.100 --> 00:39:35.670
So that, there's something
going on there potentially.

00:39:35.670 --> 00:39:40.261
So that might mean that
there's not the kind of shared

00:39:40.261 --> 00:39:44.139
division that needs to
be present in order for

00:39:44.139 --> 00:39:48.747
that team to function as
effectively as they need to.

00:39:48.747 --> 00:39:54.460
The other thing that can happen
is people just got really busy.

00:39:54.460 --> 00:39:59.690
I've seen a lot of teams where there's
just not very much communication

00:39:59.690 --> 00:40:04.640
from one Location and it's because there's

00:40:04.640 --> 00:40:09.790
a lot going on that we don't even
know about at that location.

00:40:09.790 --> 00:40:14.735
So it's another case where I think
it's always good to if you can,

00:40:14.735 --> 00:40:19.857
send a text message or pick up the phone
during their day time hours and

00:40:19.857 --> 00:40:25.711
just check in with somebody that you feel
like you've got a good rapport with.

00:40:25.711 --> 00:40:29.922
And say, hey you know, just checking in,
you know what's going on over there.

00:40:29.922 --> 00:40:33.788
Wanted to see if there's
anything we should know about,

00:40:33.788 --> 00:40:38.700
what's happening in your world there
right now, in order to get a better

00:40:38.700 --> 00:40:43.310
sense of why they might not be
responding as quickly as you'd hope.

00:40:43.310 --> 00:40:44.620
&gt;&gt; Sounds like good advice.

00:40:44.620 --> 00:40:50.160
Another person is asking you to clarify
what team reflection could look like.

00:40:51.450 --> 00:40:53.010
&gt;&gt; Right, yeah,

00:40:53.010 --> 00:40:58.010
so team reflection, oftentimes it's
just a really informal process.

00:40:58.010 --> 00:41:02.250
So getting team members in the room and

00:41:02.250 --> 00:41:06.320
just asking the question,
how are things going?

00:41:06.320 --> 00:41:09.800
Or getting in the room either virtually or
in person.

00:41:09.800 --> 00:41:13.660
It's good to have it happen in person,
but oftentimes that is not possible.

00:41:13.660 --> 00:41:18.700
Better to have it on video than just
audio when you're doing these kinds

00:41:18.700 --> 00:41:22.760
of reflections because there's additional
cues where you can see people's facial

00:41:22.760 --> 00:41:27.600
expressions and gestures can be really
useful in a reflective kind of activity.

00:41:29.985 --> 00:41:34.115
And you might want to use a process where

00:41:34.115 --> 00:41:38.755
people are able to write down
on Post-It notes or something

00:41:38.755 --> 00:41:43.635
what they think is going well and
where they think the opportunities are for

00:41:43.635 --> 00:41:49.680
improvement in either their performance or
in their group process.

00:41:50.880 --> 00:41:53.530
And then talk through those.

00:41:54.790 --> 00:42:01.350
And ideally have an open
conversation where people

00:42:01.350 --> 00:42:06.440
can articulate what it is that is working
for them and is not working for them.

00:42:06.440 --> 00:42:08.450
And then identify some strategies.

00:42:08.450 --> 00:42:12.430
What can we do in the next
week in order to help us

00:42:12.430 --> 00:42:15.310
improve on this particular element?

00:42:15.310 --> 00:42:21.450
And again, having a really open,
not rushed kind of contact.

00:42:21.450 --> 00:42:26.300
And if it's done regularly,
people get accustomed to doing it.

00:42:28.400 --> 00:42:30.410
&gt;&gt; You've talked a lot
about building trust early.

00:42:30.410 --> 00:42:33.030
We're getting several
questions on how do you

00:42:33.030 --> 00:42:35.040
back up from a team that's
become dysfunctional.

00:42:35.040 --> 00:42:39.870
So how do you rebuild trust when it's been
shattered and respect is nonexistent?

00:42:41.930 --> 00:42:45.170
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that is really challenging.

00:42:45.170 --> 00:42:53.553
I think my inclination would be to go with
one of the other two boxes on the chart.

00:42:53.553 --> 00:42:59.840
Psychological safety has also probably
been undermined on that team.

00:42:59.840 --> 00:43:05.250
And people may have lost sight
of why they need one another and

00:43:05.250 --> 00:43:08.860
why they want to build trust and
work together.

00:43:08.860 --> 00:43:14.670
So I would probably start with
the shared identity piece and

00:43:14.670 --> 00:43:19.370
really helping people to see why
this whole group is in the room,

00:43:19.370 --> 00:43:21.530
why they depend upon each other and

00:43:21.530 --> 00:43:26.250
why it's really important to
rebuild the bonds that they have.

00:43:26.250 --> 00:43:32.520
And I think it's important to name it,
too, to say, look, trust has broken down.

00:43:32.520 --> 00:43:36.674
A whole variety of reasons for
why that might've happened.

00:43:36.674 --> 00:43:43.561
Let's use some techniques

00:43:43.561 --> 00:43:51.060
to rebuild that and create a higher
level of psychological safety.

00:43:51.060 --> 00:43:54.060
And again, both with this question and
the previous question,

00:43:54.060 --> 00:43:58.510
the leader modeling is
really important in this.

00:43:58.510 --> 00:44:06.410
So the leader saying we've
had some breakdowns in trust.

00:44:06.410 --> 00:44:09.010
I hold some of the responsibility for
this.

00:44:09.010 --> 00:44:11.080
I want to rebuild it.

00:44:11.080 --> 00:44:15.380
And with psychological safety,
the leader showing some

00:44:16.430 --> 00:44:19.580
vulnerability, talking about mistakes so
forth.

00:44:19.580 --> 00:44:24.560
All of that openness and
it's really challenging because when

00:44:24.560 --> 00:44:29.290
trust isn't there, the openness is
hard and it can be misinterpreted.

00:44:29.290 --> 00:44:35.060
So you kind of have to take baby
steps in order to help rebuild and

00:44:35.060 --> 00:44:41.860
sometimes it does help to get a
facilitator in if trust is really damaged.

00:44:41.860 --> 00:44:47.340
It can help to get a mediator in that
can help facilitate those conversations.

00:44:47.340 --> 00:44:50.000
&gt;&gt; Here's a question that's
addressing language issues.

00:44:50.000 --> 00:44:52.640
I'm an American but
my team is located in Brazil.

00:44:52.640 --> 00:44:56.500
While my Portuguese is usually adequate,
there are times when I sense frustration

00:44:56.500 --> 00:44:59.620
on the part of my colleagues when
I ask them to repeat things.

00:44:59.620 --> 00:45:02.840
How would you advise team members
to be more effective communicators

00:45:02.840 --> 00:45:05.670
when the primary language used for
business isn't their first language?

00:45:07.310 --> 00:45:08.740
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a great question.

00:45:10.830 --> 00:45:16.475
Language is really challenging and
that's another segment

00:45:16.475 --> 00:45:21.676
that's covered in the course,
language challenges,

00:45:21.676 --> 00:45:26.888
both professional languages and
national languages.

00:45:26.888 --> 00:45:32.319
Couple things on that,
one is that we need to recognize, and

00:45:32.319 --> 00:45:38.400
this isn't always recognized,
that fluent does not mean fluent.

00:45:38.400 --> 00:45:43.730
When somebody is doing
business in a language that is

00:45:43.730 --> 00:45:49.665
their non-native language,
it is more challenging for them.

00:45:49.665 --> 00:45:53.780
It takes more cognitive effort.

00:45:53.780 --> 00:45:58.880
There might be vocabulary that
they don't have access to.

00:45:58.880 --> 00:46:03.040
They may have an accent,
they mispronounce things and so forth.

00:46:03.040 --> 00:46:08.340
And oftentimes, native speakers,
and this is particularly true of

00:46:08.340 --> 00:46:14.230
native English speakers, in part because
of the way our cultures operate as well.

00:46:14.230 --> 00:46:17.860
We tend not to be as patient
as we could be with that.

00:46:17.860 --> 00:46:22.930
So I think part of it is just
building an appreciation on both

00:46:22.930 --> 00:46:27.510
sides for what it means and
what the experience

00:46:27.510 --> 00:46:32.650
is of somebody who is having to
operate in a non-native language.

00:46:32.650 --> 00:46:39.250
And what the experience is of
somebody who is a native language

00:46:39.250 --> 00:46:44.252
speaker of the chosen language,
the lingua franca and

00:46:44.252 --> 00:46:49.410
having to sort of slow
down their processes

00:46:49.410 --> 00:46:54.700
in order to work
effectively in those teams.

00:46:54.700 --> 00:46:58.829
So, in particular, with your question,

00:46:58.829 --> 00:47:03.990
I think part of it is just
letting the team members that

00:47:03.990 --> 00:47:10.645
you're working with in Brazil know
that you are trying really hard,

00:47:10.645 --> 00:47:15.144
that you are attempting
to speak Portuguese.

00:47:15.144 --> 00:47:20.202
But you don't have the same
level of fluency and

00:47:20.202 --> 00:47:25.008
that you really appreciate
their patients in

00:47:25.008 --> 00:47:30.321
allowing you to occasionally
not be as articulate

00:47:30.321 --> 00:47:35.405
as you would be in your native language.

00:47:35.405 --> 00:47:39.340
So a lot of this is about
perspective taking and

00:47:39.340 --> 00:47:42.280
understanding what
people are experiencing.

00:47:42.280 --> 00:47:44.880
&gt;&gt; So
you were talking about harsh attribution.

00:47:44.880 --> 00:47:49.430
One of our participants is saying they see
issues of harsh attribution happening,

00:47:49.430 --> 00:47:51.540
in this case, between male and
female colleagues,

00:47:51.540 --> 00:47:53.850
which is escalating very quickly.

00:47:53.850 --> 00:47:54.750
How do they fix that?

00:47:59.363 --> 00:48:00.777
&gt;&gt; Interesting.

00:48:00.777 --> 00:48:03.612
Harsh attribution between male and
female colleagues.

00:48:03.612 --> 00:48:06.719
So I'm just kind of thinking through this.

00:48:08.912 --> 00:48:13.134
It sounds like it's an us
versus them problem.

00:48:13.134 --> 00:48:19.309
We tend to see more harsh attributions

00:48:19.309 --> 00:48:24.907
when people see people as others.

00:48:24.907 --> 00:48:29.499
So it's a little hard to know without
knowing more about the situation,

00:48:29.499 --> 00:48:34.259
but I think the core of the problem is
that there's some grouping going on.

00:48:34.259 --> 00:48:42.487
And for some reason, there is the women
are feeling ostracized by the men or

00:48:42.487 --> 00:48:48.102
vice versa and
that needs to be resolved in order for

00:48:48.102 --> 00:48:52.558
these harsh attributions to diminish.

00:48:52.558 --> 00:48:58.837
My guess is that for some reason, there
isn't a sense of psychological safety for

00:48:58.837 --> 00:49:05.038
even the men or the women in this group,
so that might be something to talk about.

00:49:05.038 --> 00:49:12.518
It might be worth having a conversation
about what it is that's going on and

00:49:12.518 --> 00:49:19.542
there also potentially there could
be competition that's going on.

00:49:19.542 --> 00:49:25.135
There might be a feeling of
powerlessness in one group or

00:49:25.135 --> 00:49:28.592
the other that is leading to this.

00:49:28.592 --> 00:49:33.598
So I would try to dig a little bit
deeper to investigate what the source is

00:49:33.598 --> 00:49:39.123
of this division, this subgrouping
that's happening between the genders.

00:49:39.123 --> 00:49:40.114
And sometimes,

00:49:40.114 --> 00:49:45.330
it can actually be something in
the content of what you're working on.

00:49:45.330 --> 00:49:51.970
For example, if the women are responsible
for a particular part of the project and

00:49:51.970 --> 00:49:55.990
the men are responsible for
another particular part of the project.

00:49:55.990 --> 00:49:58.980
That can set up this dynamic, as well.

00:50:00.030 --> 00:50:05.543
&gt;&gt; And asking here for recommendations
on virtual team building exercises,

00:50:05.543 --> 00:50:10.803
what are the some of the kinds of things
teens can do to build that trust?

00:50:10.803 --> 00:50:11.603
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah.

00:50:11.603 --> 00:50:16.865
So a lot of people, what they
want to do is get everybody together

00:50:16.865 --> 00:50:22.440
in a neutral location for
a couple of days of team building.

00:50:22.440 --> 00:50:25.220
I am actually not a fan of that at all.

00:50:25.220 --> 00:50:28.720
If you've got a lot of money and
a lot of time, great.

00:50:28.720 --> 00:50:32.226
Something to do, but I don't think it,

00:50:32.226 --> 00:50:37.081
the half life is relatively
short of those activities.

00:50:37.081 --> 00:50:40.875
And the reason the half life
is short is because you're not

00:50:40.875 --> 00:50:43.972
getting at the core issues
that get in the way and

00:50:43.972 --> 00:50:48.251
the court issues are really the work,
and how people do the work.

00:50:48.251 --> 00:50:52.145
And when you're off team building,
that's not what people are doing.

00:50:52.145 --> 00:50:53.631
You're getting to know one another.

00:50:53.631 --> 00:50:55.892
You're understanding personalities and
so forth.

00:50:55.892 --> 00:50:56.713
So it's good.

00:50:56.713 --> 00:51:01.701
I'm not saying it's not a good thing,
but I'm a real advocate for

00:51:01.701 --> 00:51:05.889
having people travel to
the location of the other site or

00:51:05.889 --> 00:51:08.221
sites they're working with.

00:51:08.221 --> 00:51:12.912
And not just the manager, but
the team members travel, so

00:51:12.912 --> 00:51:18.179
that they can see the context in
which one another are working with

00:51:18.179 --> 00:51:24.731
the example from Frankfurt where their
innovation approach is very different.

00:51:24.731 --> 00:51:29.419
It's really good to go and
actually sit side-by-side and

00:51:29.419 --> 00:51:33.730
see how people are working,
and who talks to them, and

00:51:33.730 --> 00:51:36.652
what their office space looks like.

00:51:36.652 --> 00:51:38.500
And what they do for lunch and

00:51:38.500 --> 00:51:41.821
the cultural context in
which they're embedded.

00:51:41.821 --> 00:51:46.595
That has a much more profound
effect on collaboration

00:51:46.595 --> 00:51:51.594
going forward than team
building kinds of activities.

00:51:51.594 --> 00:51:53.393
&gt;&gt; Great.
Thank you so much, Pamela.

00:51:53.393 --> 00:51:55.529
We want to thank you for your time today.

00:51:55.529 --> 00:51:57.425
We hope you enjoy the hour and

00:51:57.425 --> 00:52:02.486
we encourage you to check out all of
our offerings at scpd.stanford.edu.

00:52:02.486 --> 00:52:06.007
And again, don't forget that you'll be
receiving a recording of this webinar to

00:52:06.007 --> 00:52:07.804
share with your friends and colleagues.

00:52:07.804 --> 00:52:08.787
Have a great day.

